# The flip-flop: a master/slave pair of locked repeaters, acting on the falling
# clock edge. The clock enters at N and weakly powers a stone; torch T1 on it
# gives NOT clock to lock K1, so the master M1 is open while the clock is high
# and holds while it is low; torch T2 on a second stone gives the clock back,
# four ticks late, to lock K2, so the slave M2 is open while the clock is low.
# At the falling edge the master closes and, two ticks later, the slave opens
# and Q takes the master's value. The lock and the master's last change can land
# on the same tick, which is safe either way round: a locked repeater ignores a
# pending tick. The `timing` figures were measured in stage 8 (see PLAN.md):
# data must be stable from 1 tick before the falling edge to 5 after it; Q
# follows 8 ticks after the edge; the clock must stay high and low 4 ticks each
# and have a period of at least 12.
tile dff
size 10 4 5
port W in 0 1 2
port N in 2 1 0
port E out 9 1 2
function dff
delay 8
edge falling
timing setup 1 hold 5 clk_to_q 8 min_high 4 min_low 4 min_period 12

legend
  G minecraft:glass
  # minecraft:stone
  - minecraft:redstone_wire
  > minecraft:redstone_wall_torch[facing=east]
  K minecraft:repeater[facing=north,delay=1]
  M minecraft:repeater[facing=west,delay=1]

layer 0
  ..G.......
  ..GGGGGG..
  GG..G..GGG
  .GGGGGGGG.
  ..........
layer 1
  ..-.......
  ..#>--#>..
  --..K..K--
  .---M--M-.
  ..........
layer 2
  ..........
  ..........
  ..........
  ..........
  ..........
layer 3
  ..........
  ..........
  ..........
  ..........
  ..........
